Writing a basic kernel
inux kernel basic project with code
Building a Linux Kernel Module (LKM) is the standard "Hello World" project for kernel development. Unlike standard C programs, these run in kernel space, meaning they have direct hardware access but no access to standard libraries like stdio.h. 
1. The Kernel Module Code (hello.c)
Create a file named hello.c. This simple script logs a message when the module is loaded and another when it is removed. 
c
#include <linux/init.h>   // Macros for module_init and module_exit
#include <linux/module.h> // Core header for loading modules
#include <linux/kernel.h> // Contains KERN_INFO log level

// Metadata for the kernel
MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
MODULE_AUTHOR("Your Name");
MODULE_DESCRIPTION("A Basic Hello World Kernel Module");

// Called when the module is loaded (insmod)
static int __init hello_init(void) {
    printk(KERN_INFO "Hello, Kernel! Module loaded successfully.\n");
    return 0; // 0 indicates success
}

// Called when the module is removed (rmmod)
static void __exit hello_exit(void) {
    printk(KERN_INFO "Goodbye, Kernel! Module removed.\n");
}

module_init(hello_init);
module_exit(hello_exit);
Use code with caution.

2. The Makefile
Kernel modules require a specific build process using the kbuild system. Create a file named Makefile (capital 'M') in the same directory. 
makefile
obj-m += hello.o

all:
	make -C /lib/modules/$(shell uname -r)/build M=$(PWD) modules

clean:
	make -C /lib/modules/$(shell uname -r)/build M=$(PWD) clean
Use code with caution.

3. Build and Run Steps
To test your project, follow these terminal commands: 
Compile: Run make. This generates a hello.ko (Kernel Object) file.
Load: Run sudo insmod hello.ko. This inserts the module into the running kernel.
Verify: Run dmesg | tail. You should see "Hello, Kernel!" in the logs.
List: Run lsmod | grep hello to confirm it is active.
Unload: Run sudo rmmod hello.
Verify Removal: Run dmesg | tail again to see the "Goodbye" message. 
Core Development Tips
Safety: Never test new kernel code on a production machine. Use a VirtualBox VM or QEMU to avoid system crashes (kernel panics).
Logging: Since you can't use printf, use printk. View these logs in real-time with dmesg -w.
